  <text>
    <body>
      <div type="transcription" xml:id="transcription_aad_0187" xml:lang="en">
          <div type="letter">
        <pb facs="https://iiif.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/aad/aad_00562/" ed="1r" type="sheet"/>
            <div type="letter_message" hand="#type_black_mayer">
              <opener>
                <dateline>
                  <date>
                    <lb/>May 7 th l964
                    <lb/>Christi Himmelfahrt
                  </date>
                </dateline>
                <salute>
                  <lb/>Dearest <rs type="person" ref="#amp_person_1">friend</rs> - 
                </salute>
              </opener>
                <p>
                  <lb/>you cannot imagine how happy I was with your letter - 
                  <lb/>I had been worrying about you, what with all your work
                  <lb/>in the last months, and certainly all kind of cares -, and
                  <lb/>to know that you are back in your 'nest' was quite a
                  <lb/>relief! I do hope you have fine Pfingstwetter and found
                  <lb/>everything in good order and enjoy the countryside and
                  <lb/>peace. Do you have anyone to look after you? Does Chester
                  <lb/>come back? or are you going to take up the life of an
                  <lb/>eremite? ( I like it better than hermit ) Your haiku poem
                  <lb/>from Iceland is wonderful and very amusing ,with undertones.
                  <lb/>It must have been in many ways a strange experience. I was
                  <lb/>glad you had a dog-friend. And what shall I tell you about
                  <lb/>my birthday poem. I love it, and have deciphered most of 
                  <lb/>the allusions. Isabelle explained the gases to me,which
                  <lb/>I had not quite got. And I have to confess that I did not
                  <lb/>know that the Scherzo has ever been called the governess-
                  <lb/>scherzo.( although I played it often when a governess in
                  <lb/>Bohemia ) That you brought my father's Sand Uhr in, is
                  <lb/>so lovely,the whole atmosphere of my childhood is in the 
                  <lb/>first two lines. And your deep understanding at the end..
                  <lb/>Yes, my dear, these twenty-five years of our friendship have
                  <lb/>meant so much to me, more than I could ever have told you,
                  <lb/>but you must have felt it. I think of them with gratitude:
                  <lb/>you have done so much for me, I feel very much as your pupil,
                </p>
            </div>
        <pb facs="https://iiif.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/aad/aad_00563/" ed="1v" type="sheet"/>
            <div>
              <ab hand="#hand_blue_unident_0187">
                <lb/>64
              </ab>
            </div>
            <div type="letter_message" hand="#type_black_mayer">
              <p>
                <lb/>how often did you " strike a match to my brain " and not
                <lb/>only that. You have confirmed me and encouraged me so often,
                <lb/>and I do not think I could go on working so boldly as I do
                <lb/>without your open and tacit understanding.
              </p>
              <p>
                <lb/>I am already deep in my new translation.It is great fun
                <lb/>to play again with the German language, but the book is
                <lb/>difficult ,not the plain prose but the dialogue and the 
                <lb/>quickness and irony in it. Hard to get that in German. But
                <lb/>it is surprising how the words and colloquialisms rush into
                <lb/>one's memory from all sides and corners of your brain while
                <lb/>writing. I do hope to get it all right. I try to live 
                <lb/>like an eremite, and succeed. The weather is just now
                <lb/>lovely, and I sit often in the Park and prepare in my head
                <lb/>a chapter, run upstairs and type it. I go out almost never,
                <lb/>was only once in the Goethe Haus to hear Erich Heller
                <lb/>who spoke very well and witty about Hegel and the attitude
                <lb/>of the Germans toward Shakespeare. I met there, again Dwight Mcdon.
                <lb/>who loves the Elective Affs. and wants to write something 
                <lb/>about the book. Pazienza. Michael has had, and still
                <lb/>has a very hard time.His old Rector died suddenly ( in the
                <lb/>Met, during the first act of Tosca) and M. has to run the
                <lb/>whole parish. No decisions as yet. I met the Bishop at
                <lb/>the Funeral Mass ,and he said to me wonderful things about
                <lb/>Michael. We are wondering now... ..
              </p>
              <closer>
                <salute><lb/>Much much love,Wystan dear.</salute>
              </closer>
              <postscript>
                <ab>
                  <lb/>P.S. I shall certainly get the Krauss translations.
                  <lb/>The 'Merkur" brought one of your Shaksp. Essays, not too
                  <lb/>bad, but not Auden's style.
                </ab>
              </postscript>
              <closer>
                <signed hand="#hand_blue_mayer">
                  <lb/>Elizabeth
                </signed>
              </closer>
            </div>
          </div>
      </div>
    </body>
